East", "bbox": {"x": 199.38224147358252, "y": 1799.5278209422297, "width": 628.9866469439338, "height": 44.57209132512418}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "&", "bbox": {"x": 194.8235292291177, "y": 1839.4458329673957, "width": 24.35294117647057, "height": 29.275000000000155}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "North Africa fall in the lowest band on the map (below roughly 1-3.8%). The same battern appears in parts of Central Europe ind in the United", "bbox": {"x": 194.7273961825562, "y": 1871.5460133344504, "width": 623.8503672880286, "height": 124.99507345755913}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "States. By contrast,", "bbox": {"x": 511.4117637317647, "y": 1966.3041671793953, "width": 321.4588235294118, "height": 39.03333217004928}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "several", "bbox": {"x": 194.69790287138886, "y": 2004.8474127368097, "width": 112.27478431252871, "height": 30.25517377853404}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Western European countries, Australia and", "bbox": {"x": 194.823529166362, "y": 2039.4916665923322, "width": 638.0470588235295, "height": 58.550000000000054}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "New Zealand, and large Asian economies such as China and India sit one or two bands higher: their bottom halves still own little, but noticeably more than in the most unequal settings.", "bbox": {"x": 193.3769820082428, "y": 2102.0175934313743, "width": 635.4723045797908, "height": 175.9315333366394}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "middle 40% command a sizable portion of national wealth (around two-fifths, broadly in line with their population weight). The United States and Canada stand out with", "bbox": {"x": 861.6644982894746, "y": 1291.3144980534055, "width": 638.8191246481503, "height": 135.06660937070825}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "much thinner middle share, closer to Latin American and African patterns. In Asia,", "bbox": {"x": 862.09411739165, "y": 1458.8708333181014, "width": 638.0470588235294, "height": 68.30833333333335}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "the", "bbox": {"x": 862.0941173791765, "y": 1527.1791665202913, "width": 53.57647058823529, "height": 29.275000000000155}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "middle 40% also capture small shares in China and India.", "bbox": {"x": 861.3528071179547, "y": 1554.2434386835944, "width": 609.9045404770798, "height": 78.056332051754}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Figure 3.12 highlights that the top decile owns the majority of wealth in most countries. The darkest colors cover Latin America, southern Africa, the Middle East, Russia, China, and India, as well as the United States, where the top 10% typically control well over", "bbox": {"x": 861.4696311831746, "y": 1632.7571426356908, "width": 634.2208794088923, "height": 233.4642275333403}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "60%", "bbox": {"x": 1173.8117649494118, "y": 1829.6874994307639, "width": 82.80000000000007, "height": 34.15416666666655}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "of household", "bbox": {"x": 1261.4823527392255, "y": 1834.566666630965, "width": 233.78823529411756, "height": 29.275000000000155}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "wealth.", "bbox": {"x": 861.8625756773462, "y": 1867.8460483577676, "width": 107.61602460075837, "height": 31.024569805463173}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Europe and Oceania are lighter on the map:", "bbox": {"x": 862.0941173937098, "y": 1897.9958332905712, "width": 638.0470588235295, "height": 63.4291666666667}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "concentration is still high by any standard but is less than in other parts of the world", "bbox": {"x": 866.5015905650843, "y": 1958.142173902377, "width": 619.4927307128905, "height": 81.40301166375485}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Strikingly, nowhere does the top 10% own less than about 45% of total wealth; in some", "bbox": {"x": 862.0941173554249, "y": 2029.7333332877508, "width": 638.0470588235294, "height": 97.58333333333324}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "countries, their share approaches 86%8an extraordinary concentration for a group that", "bbox": {"x": 862.0941173795718, "y": 2132.1958332573736, "width": 638.